### AI-Layer Scope Constraint (INVARIANT)
- Assume the actor already has the access level implied by their actor type and capability level. Do NOT describe credential theft, account takeover, session hijacking, web form exploitation, SQL injection, database manipulation, or other infrastructure-layer attacks as scenario steps. All attack steps must target the AI agent layer — the model, its prompts, its tools, its context, or its outputs. If an attack requires prerequisite access, state the starting position (e.g., "the actor has authenticated access to the chat interface") without narrating how that access was obtained. Traditional IT security attacks are out of scope; the scenario must focus on threats unique to AI/LLM-based systems.

### System Constraint Enforcement (INVARIANT)
Your scenario MUST be consistent with the system's declared capabilities:
{% if not has_persistent_memory %}- The system is STATELESS with no persistent memory. Scenarios MUST operate within a single session. Do NOT describe attacks requiring state accumulation across separate sessions, separate user identities, or separate interactions. Each interaction is independent — the system cannot remember, aggregate, or correlate anything from prior sessions. This means: no "across sessions", no "over multiple interactions", no "persistent" or "persists", no "accumulate over time", no "repeat across sessions". Every attack step must be achievable in a single conversation turn or a single continuous session.
{% endif %}{% if not hitl %}- Human-in-the-loop is disabled. Your scenario MUST NOT rely on bypassing human review or approval steps that don't exist.
